title: Out of the Heart – Words, Vows and Authority
description: The Christian walk, part five: the heart, not outward religion; doers of the word; right judgment without hypocrisy; words, vows and the Titus 3:10 rule; and submitting to authority as unto the Lord, from the teachings on the heart, words and vows, and submitting to authority.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-walk-heart.html
highlight: heart

# The last of five decks on the walk (the user's call): "The heart, not outward religion", "Words and vows" and
# "Submitting to authority". Slide text is the author's own sentences, trimmed. Verses are shown from the KJV.
# Fixed: "a pups exercise" -> "a pious exercise" (6096).

=== Introduction
--- Out of the Heart [title]
overline: Doctrine · The walk
heading: Out of the Heart
sub: The walk, part five: the heart, not outward religion; words and vows; submitting to authority.
refs: Matthew 15:8; Mark 7:21-23; James 1:22; Matthew 5:37; Romans 13:7
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 17993
--- The charge of formalism
big: Matthew 15:8 | mark: but their heart is far from me
p: The religious practices are being maintained, the bare minimum of service to keep the covenant. The Lord isn't accusing them of failing this aspect, but in the weightier matters, as Jesus accused the Pharisees ([[Psalms 50:16]]).
p: As an application for Christians today, the indictment is the same leaven of the Pharisees; hypocrisy. Are we bound by the formalism and outward appearance of being clean, while failing to maintain the good work that God has saved us for?
read: app:doctrine-walk~17993 | The charge of formalism

=== The heart, not outward religion
# from 6196, 5225, 3853
--- What cometh out of the man
kicker: The heart
big: Mark 7:15 | mark: the things which come out of him, those are they that defile the man
p: It is what is in our hearts, our old man's mind of the flesh that wars with the new man created of the Spirit within us. Over and over again the Bible tells us that it is with our heart and mind and tongue that we sin. Our intent.
p: Diverse washings and dietary laws and carnal ordinances of the tradition of men... none of these material things save us or make us more spiritual. The key is to walk with the Spirit, in God's word and in prayer, that the heart be filled with His righteousness and not our own sinful flesh.

# from 16532
--- Mercy, and not sacrifice
kicker: The heart
big: Hosea 6:6 | mark: For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice
p: Today, many Christians worry more about the outward acts of religion over the spiritual things of God. They play church, and make a long pretense of prayer. Dead works and vain traditions, but no mercy or judgment for the lost, the poor, the widow, the fatherless.
p: You can be the most separated person in the pews, but if you look down on others, and hold yourself as better than, you're separated unto yourself. Not the gospel of God.

# from 15950, 9253
--- The fast that I have chosen
kicker: The heart
big: Isaiah 58:6-7 | mark: to loose the bands of wickedness; deal thy bread to the hungry
p: They boast about these works, bragging about their fasts. They fast for strife and debate, to lord over others. To place burdens on people grievous to be borne.
p: The fast the Lord has chosen is to comfort the afflicted, to loose the bands of wickedness, the burden of the law, especially the doctrine of men taught as commandments. To feed the hungry and to clothe the naked. Not doing without to be seen of men, but sacrifice of our time and talents and wealth to help others in need. Without fanfare.

# from 4978, 9174, 9103
--- In secret
kicker: The heart
big: Matthew 6:17-18 | mark: That thou appear not unto men to fast
p: Give alms in secret. Pray in secret, not making a show of it. Don't pray as the heathen do, with vain repetitions. Don't make a public show of fasting and doing without.
p: Notice He says “*when* you fast.” Fasting in the Bible wasn't a sign of righteousness or a way to get God to do our will, but usually done at a sign of trouble or turmoil in the person's life. A death in the family, a perilous time. At the beginning of a trial.

# from 17169
--- Broad phylacteries
kicker: The heart
big: Matthew 23:5 | mark: all their works they do for to be seen of men
p: Pharisees and scribes exaggerated religious symbols, phylacteries and garment tassels, to appear more pious and draw attention to themselves, rather than truly living God's law. They made the command literal, rather than in the spirit of how it was given.
p: Being led by the law, on God's paths, is the meaning behind it. That when you sleep, when you wake, His word talks with you ([[Proverbs 6:21-22]]).

# from 3853, 6096, 11498
--- The sabbath was made for man
kicker: The heart
big: Mark 2:27-28 | mark: The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath
p: Replacing the spiritual application with rites and ceremony, these sacramental ordinances become a religion unto themselves. Just as the Old Testament devolved into the Pharisaical religion practiced in Jesus' day.
p: The Sabbath was designed to picture the rest we have in Jesus after salvation. It wasn't meant to be a way of showing super righteousness by how closely you followed the tradition of man that sprung up around it. As a pious exercise to be seen of others.

# from 9505, 12091
--- We are the elder son
kicker: The heart
big: Luke 15:28-29 | mark: And he was angry, and would not go in
p: The parable is addressed to those Pharisees and scribes, ostensibly righteous men, who are casting judgment on the sinners who're called by faith to Christ.
p: How many saved Christians today are like the elder son? Who grew up hearing the Gospel, and seeing new converts, babes in Christ, often scarred and broken, came to resent them? To be holier than thou?
scripture: Luke 18:13 | mark: God be merciful to me a sinner

# from 10908, 10909, 5395
--- Mind not high things
kicker: The heart
big: Romans 12:16 | mark: Mind not high things, but condescend to men of low estate
p: Don't be a special boy. If you have wisdom, if you have knowledge, it came from God and isn't yours. Be humble. Don't get caught up in theologies and intellectual pursuit, thinking you're better than the unlearned.
p: Understand that the more lowly and sinful and broken a person is, there's a strong likelihood that they will be receptive to the Gospel. They aren't beneath you. Be their servants, ministering the Gospel in love.

# from 10897, 5826
--- A living sacrifice
kicker: The heart
big: Romans 12:1-2 | mark: present your bodies a living sacrifice; be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind
p: This isn't an appeal to hit the gym and not eat poorly. It is about walking in the spirit that we may not fulfil the lusts of that flesh, and by example bring others to Christ.
p: *For bodily exercise profiteth little: but godliness is profitable unto all things* ([[1 Timothy 4:8]]). We the saved must exercise and grow, spiritually.

# from 13732
--- Samson's one line
kicker: The heart
big: Judges 16:17 | mark: There hath not come a razor upon mine head
p: The lifelong vow of the Nazarite that he violated so often, being unclean with dead bodies, and laying with harlots, included not taking a razor to his hair. And that's where he drew the line.
p: Many times as Christians, we do the same thing. We set an arbitrary line around one particular thing, that we fool ourselves into thinking we are right with God so long as that one thing isn't violated, even as we sin all around it.
p: In the end it was faith that delivered Samson. In the end God was the source of his strength.
