=== Doers of the word
# from 16335, 3550
--- As a very lovely song
kicker: Doers of the word
big: Ezekiel 33:32 | mark: they hear thy words, but they do them not
p: God compares their vain pleasure in hearing the word of God preached to those listening to a song. It's got a good beat, and the singer is talented, but it has no deeper meaning than that for them.
p: How many professing Christians, even in solid fundamental churches, are there just to enjoy the show? Who show up to a service, check the box, hear the word, and let it drip off them like water off a duck? Spending time with God's word should change you.
scripture: James 1:22 | mark: be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only

# from 9212, 9211, 9273
--- Built on the rock
kicker: Doers of the word
big: Luke 6:46 | mark: why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?
p: If we live by His word, which is like a foundation of rock, we will not be moved. If we don't, but lean into our own understanding, we are building on shifting sands ([[Luke 6:48-49]]).
p: What is it your heart treasures? The mercy and grace of God? Or your petty resentments and grudges? *For of the abundance of the heart his mouth speaketh* ([[Luke 6:45]]).
p: It is up to us whether we be the rock or the thorny ground, or if we are fertile soil. We have a choice ([[Luke 8:8]]).

# from 14612
--- Once you begin
kicker: Doers of the word
big: James 4:17 | mark: to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin
p: If you begin reading the Bible every day, you're admitting that you should. If you go to church once, you are admitting you should be going regularly, and now you don't have an excuse. This can be terrifying. It makes us make rationalizations. Or empty promises to “do better tomorrow.”
p: You will fail sometimes. It's ok. God's mercies are new every morning. You will be making yourself obligated to doing the right thing by acknowledging it is the right thing. That is the Spirit working in you. Accept it.

# from 8722, 5432
--- Render unto God the things that are God's
kicker: Doers of the word
big: Matthew 22:21 | mark: and unto God the things that are God’s
p: Often the focus here is mistakenly on Jesus saying to render unto Caesar what is Caesar's, which is true. But Jesus is turning the question around on those tempting Him, that their focus should be on the spiritual. They do not render unto God that which is His due.
p: We may be justified by the blood of Christ before God, but if we hide that faith under a bushel, or we bury that which we've been trusted, our faith is fruitless. Dead ([[James 2:17]]).
read: app:doctrine-walk~16335 | Doers of the word, not hearers only
read: app:doctrine-walk~9212 | Built on the rock: doers of His sayings
read: app:doctrine-walk~14612 | Once you begin, you are without excuse

=== Right judgment
# from 17777, 17418
--- Judge not, in context
kicker: Right judgment
big: Matthew 7:1-2 | mark: Judge not, that ye be not judged
p: Jesus' words are often twisted by others to mean a Christian should never judge another person, no matter how wicked they are. But we know the warning is against hypocrisy, not right judgment. *Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment* ([[John 7:24]]).
p: Rightly judge, beginning with ourselves that we may purge ourselves. *First cast out the beam out of thine own eye* ([[Matthew 7:5]]).

# from 17418
--- Another man's servant
kicker: Right judgment
big: Romans 14:4 | mark: Who art thou that judgest another man’s servant?
p: We are answerable to God. If one is falling down in some area, weak in the faith, God will correct them. Accusing them accuses Him ([[Proverbs 30:10]]).
p: It isn't an excuse to enable, and standards and church discipline still exist. But encouraging speech, edifying one another with fear and admonition of the Lord, is what we should use. Not accusations of being less holy than thou.

# from 17234, 13544, 13545
--- Hear the matter first
kicker: Right judgment
big: Proverbs 18:13 | mark: He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him
p: *He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him* ([[Proverbs 18:17]]). We should not go with the first story you hear.
p: Phinehas, zealous for the Lord at Peor, shows discernment, judgment and mercy at the altar Ed. Rather than accuse, he asks of them the altar's purpose, and he listens to their answer ([[Joshua 22:24-31]]). We need to meekly and humbly seek to know, before either grabbing a javelin or declaring peace.

# from 17299
--- No respect of persons in judgment
kicker: Right judgment
big: Proverbs 24:23-25 | mark: It is not good to have respect of persons in judgment
p: The rich or the lords among us should be subject to the same treatment as the lowly and poor. Judge a man by the words they speak, their reputation for wisdom, diligence, the honor they give their parents. Not by appearances.
p: If you pervert justice and promote the wicked and cast your lot with the rich man over the widow or fatherless, woe unto you ([[Isaiah 5:20]]). It is a blessing to call it straight and to rebuke that which is evil.
read: app:doctrine-walk~17777 | Judge not means no hypocrisy, not no judgment
read: app:doctrine-walk~13544 | Phinehas heard them before judging

=== Words and vows
# from 17706, 4664 (the Raca and ass examples kept, at the author's word)
--- The window to the heart
kicker: Words and vows
big: Matthew 12:36-37 | mark: by thy words thou shalt be justified, and by thy words thou shalt be condemned
p: Words are the window to the heart of a man. One of the most repeated commands in scripture is to watch what you say.
p: Corrupt communication isn't simply a naughty word, as if it had power to corrupt. In this context, it's the things we say in wrath and anger, envy, strife, backbiting, tale bearing, railing, false accusations. Is it edifying ([[Ephesians 4:29]])?
p: Jesus wasn't using corrupt communication when he said “Raca.” When scripture says “ass,” or “pisseth.” Overly legalistic types lose their mind at a stray vulgarity while accepting hypocrisy, backbiting and railing, being holier than thou.

# from 13160, 11567, 12466
--- Swear not at all
kicker: Words and vows
big: Matthew 5:37 | mark: let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay
p: A just man doesn't need to prove by vow what his word is, because by being honest in all things, and keeping it to yes or no, and meaning it, we uphold the spirit of the law. Men will believe us or not ([[James 5:12]]).
p: We are already called to live consecrated lives in service of the Lord. Separated unto the Gospel. Dedicated to the purposes of God without vow, but as His.

# from 13160, 15146, 15147
--- Don't make a vow you cannot keep
kicker: Words and vows
big: Ecclesiastes 5:5 | mark: Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay
p: People who “swear to God,” who make promises to God they cannot keep, need to be careful. God is very clear that He will exact the price for your vow ([[Deuteronomy 23:21-22]]).
p: All Judah took an oath in their zeal, but the high places were not taken away ([[2 Chronicles 15:12-17]]). None of us are perfect. Better to rely on God's mercy, than make promises in our excitement, that we in the flesh cannot keep.

# from 17323, 17326, 17274, 17181
--- Answer a fool
kicker: Words and vows
big: Proverbs 26:4-5 | mark: Answer not a fool according to his folly; Answer a fool according to his folly
p: When a fool is arguing foolishness, bickering back and forth makes you as much a fool, no matter how correct you are. But to not answer leaves him *wise in his own conceit*. The instruction then is to rebuke, not argue. To correct with authority, from scripture especially.
p: Jesus' words against giving the word of God to dogs and swine, the sodomites and the reprobate, is an escalation to the proverb ([[Matthew 7:6]]). When we are out soul-winning, never get into arguments with the unreceptive or the mockers. You simply let them oppose themselves and move on. Shake off the dust ([[Matthew 10:14]]).

# from 6041, 17011, 5877
--- The Titus 3:10 rule
kicker: Words and vows
big: Titus 3:10-11 | mark: A man that is an heretick after the first and second admonition reject
p: Debates and silly theological questions about the law help no one. They can be a snare and a stumbling block to those seeking truth ([[Titus 3:9]]). If you've shown them clear scripture, two times, and they still insist, then shake the dust.
p: Special boys need the person they are “debating” to fall into the trap of defending the false representation of their own argument ([[Job 35:2]]). The second they enter into putting words in your mouth, they are in bad faith. Be satisfied, instead of having to prove them wrong. That's your pride, and it helps no one. (I have fallen in to this trap. It's so tempting.)

# from 7022, 6303, 17329
--- Meekness as strength
kicker: Words and vows
big: Romans 10:2 | mark: they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge
p: Rather we should correct them with love from the scripture they're misusing. Many of these people are well meaning. Pray that God opens their eyes ([[Romans 10:17]]).
p: When we spiral in anger and get drawn into debate, all we do is give them the hook to hang us with. Jesus silenced them by their own hypocrisy ([[Mark 11:29-33]]).
p: Stay out of contentions that don't involve you. It is like one that *taketh a dog by the ears* ([[Proverbs 26:17]]).
read: app:doctrine-walk~13160 | Don't make a vow you cannot keep
read: app:doctrine-walk~17323 | Answer a fool according to his folly
read: app:doctrine-walk~6041 | Reject a heretic after two admonitions

=== Submitting to authority
# from 10525, 10526
--- As Christ submitted
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Philippians 2:7-8 | mark: he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death
p: As Christ, who thought it not robbery to be equal with God, submitted Himself to the will of the Father, so we must submit ourselves to doing what we are created for; giving pleasure to God ([[Revelation 4:11]]).
p: Husbands submit to Christ. Wives submitting to husbands are submitting to Christ. Children honoring parents, servants working diligently, masters who rule justly, members of a nation submitting to authority, all are serving Christ ([[Ephesians 6:6-9]]).

# from 10968, 16005
--- The spirit of rebellion
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: 1 Samuel 15:23 | mark: For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft
p: “But I don't like it. I don't like being told what to do. I am obviously better than my parents, than my boss, than my rulers.” The same spirit of rebellion that Satan loosed into the world.
p: Many people today hate the idea of submitting to authority. Men refusing to join a church and submit themselves to a pastor. Who slack off at work, constantly disrespecting their bosses. This encourages wives to rebel against husbands, and children against parents. Ultimately, it is rebellion against God.

# from 16005, 10968
--- Daniel's meek appeal
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Daniel 1:8 | mark: Daniel purposed in his heart that he would not defile himself
p: But what if authority over you asks you to do something that would offend God? Daniel shows us how it is done. Meek and faithful, he doesn't rebel against the authority in his life, and yet doesn't compromise his faith. He instead asks permission ([[Daniel 1:12]]).
p: There's never an occasion to partake in another man's sin. There is always a way out, even if that way is to sacrifice yourself, that those who witness understand the right path ([[1 Corinthians 10:13]]).

# from 10968
--- We are all here
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Acts 16:28 | mark: Do thyself no harm: for we are all here
p: When an earthquake came and tare down the walls of the prison, Paul and Silas remained, obedient to the charge. And the Philippian jailer said, *Sirs, what must I do to be saved?* ([[Acts 16:30-31]])
p: The church of the Philippians was born and grew, and did mighty things for God, because of the obedience of Paul and Silas. To God and to government, even the government that unjustly persecuted them.

# from 17533, 16811
--- Yielding pacifieth great offences
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Ecclesiastes 10:4 | mark: yielding pacifieth great offences
p: If a ruler comes against you, justly or unjustly, it is better to yield than to flee or fight. David, pursued by Saul, had opportunity to kill the king, and refused. Daniel and his friends, Joseph in Egypt.
p: Mordecai did not bow to Haman ([[Esther 3:2-5]]). The Jews in captivity were told to *seek the peace of the city* ([[Jeremiah 29:7]]). The entire affair could have been avoided by simply and meekly bowing. His rebellion seems borne of arrogance and pride.

# from 10969, 5432, 10180
--- Tribute to whom tribute is due
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Romans 13:7 | mark: tribute to whom tribute is due; honour to whom honour
p: Rendering to Caesar what is Caesar's is a trifling thing. Paying custom and tribute; taxes, is nothing. God provided us what we have and will again. Even from the mouth of a fish ([[Matthew 17:27]]).
p: God can use unsaved people, people who are wrong on doctrine but in position of authority. Moses heeded Jethro ([[Exodus 18:24]]). Submit with meekness and faith, always keeping God as the head, that we don't join in another man's sin.

# from 10967, 13088
--- Learning to serve that we may rule
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Deuteronomy 17:19-20 | mark: That his heart be not lifted up above his brethren
p: We who are saved are heirs to the Kingdom, to rule and reign with Christ. We learn to minister that we may rule justly. How can He trust us with much when we squandered the talents we were given?
p: God told whomever would be king to make a handwritten copy of the law, that he may know it, and serve with a humble heart. We are not to turn either to the left or the right, but to maintain the straight paths He has set before us.

# from 17245, 16993, 16998, 17007, 17282
--- The hoary head
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: Leviticus 19:32 | mark: Thou shalt rise up before the hoary head
p: Young men, do not be as Elihu ([[Job 32:6]]). He waited on the elders, but not out of respect, but to bide his time so he could give his opinion. He is a special boy. You aren't a special boy, and you have limited understanding. Have real humility.
p: Rehoboam *forsook the counsel of the old men* ([[1 Kings 12:8]]), and a kingdom was lost. Young Christian men especially should seek to learn from their elders, not mock them.

# from 16990, 16991
--- Gravity before office
kicker: Submitting to authority
big: 1 Timothy 3:10 | mark: let these also first be proved
p: Respected men of faith in the church don't put on this gravity like a coat when they get named. You must be these things before you are ever even considered for leadership.
p: If you are a Christian man in your middle age, and aren't mentoring young men in your church, give an honest assessment between you and God. You might never hold a title, but your quiet strength and aged wisdom can still be used mightily by God.
